summary:
Beyond the Void is an interactive 3D website that takes visitors to the seven objects NASA left behind on the Moon and Mars, from Surveyor 3 (1967) to the Ingenuity helicopter (2021).
A scroll-driven story flies the camera from Earth to each landing site. Free-explore mode lets visitors spin the Moon and Mars, click an object to see it up close, and read its file:
what it was built to do and what we learned from it;
a slider comparing a photo from its landing with a photo from orbit;
a to-scale size drawing;
up to 30-second narrated video;
a quick quiz.

A timeline from 1967 to 2021 shows the hardware building up on the two worlds. Four machines appear as real NASA 3D models.

The challenge asks for stories that turn discarded equipment into something people care about. Our answer treats each machine as a character with a mission, an ending and a legacy, not as space junk. These “abandoned” landers and rovers are still historic sites. Their work shapes how we explore today. Telling their stories helps young people, our main audience of ages 8–18, see them as the start of exploration, not its leftovers.
